  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was a study in capacity constraints and divergent store health.\n\nPort Washington fired on all cylinders: $630 revenue on just 3 bather hours (well below the 4.5h trailing average) signals strong demand and exceptional retail momentum\u2014$395 in retail alone, up 25% on just one incremental transaction. Core grooming jumped 50% week-over-week despite zero groomer appointments, which means your bathers are converting walk-ins and retail is carrying the day. Add-on attach improved to 67% from 50%, so your team is executing on upsell discipline. You should be proud of this. MTD Port Washington sits at $55,721, just 1.2% below DOW-adjusted expectation\u2014essentially on plan. The real signal: visit-cadence drift is reversing (12+ week bucket down 2.2 points), which is early evidence that retention is improving.\n\nHicksville is the concern. Yesterday was a retail-only day with zero grooming activity and revenue collapsed 60% despite being flat MTD. More worrying: the 12+ week visit gap is growing (10.2% to 12.6%), classic stealth churn. You're running just 2 groomer-days per week at Hicksville versus 4.4 at Port Washington, and it's showing\u2014productivity per groomer is $425 vs $495 at Port Washington. The lapse pool of 84 customers represents $61k in dormant annual value. This location needs a capacity or scheduling reset, not a performance blame.\n\nAcross both stores, bather hours are misaligned with groomer scheduling. Port Washington has 7 bathers today but only 5 groomers; Hicksville has zero bathers despite 1 groomer. This is bottleneck architecture.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Port Washington has 5 groomers + 7.0h bathers today and 6 groomers + 0h bathers tomorrow; Hicksville has 1 groomer + 0h bathers today and 2 groomers + 0h bathers tomorrow; across the next 7 days you have 47 total groomer-days scheduled (Port 34, Hicksville 13) and 43 bather hours (all Port).**"
